<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Empress Dowager Cixi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1835">1835</a>–<a href="/years/1908">1908</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Cixi · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-aisin-gioro">House of Aisin Gioro</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Empress Dowager Cixi was the dominant figure at the Qing court from the 1860s until her death in 1908, ruling as regent for the Tongzhi and Guangxu emperors. She supported some limited modernisation but suppressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days' Reform</a> of 1898 and backed the Boxers in 1900. Her rule is often associated with the late decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi.jpg" alt="Empress Dowager Cixi" width="262" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Yu Shuinling · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Ci-Xi_Imperial_Dowager_Empress_(9.2).PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cixi was born Xingzhen of the Yehe Nara clan on 29 November 1835, probably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. Her father, Huizheng, belonged to the Bordered Blue Banner and held the title of third class duke. In 1851 she was among 61 candidates presented for selection as wives of the Xianfeng Emperor, and she was chosen as a concubine. She entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a> on 26 June 1852 as a consort of the sixth rank, styled Noble Lady Lan, and in 1856 she gave birth to the emperor's son, Zaichun.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Xianfeng died in 1861 her son became the Tongzhi Emperor, and she became co-empress dowager with his widow, Empress Dowager Ci'an. While the court waited at Rehe for an auspicious time to carry the coffin to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, she conspired with officials and imperial relatives, and the two empresses ousted the eight regents the late emperor had appointed. Ruling from behind the curtain, they issued edicts for the boy emperor and changed his regnal title from Qixiang to Tongzhi. Cixi oversaw the Tongzhi Restoration, rejecting Western political institutions while supporting technological and military modernisation.</p><p>After Tongzhi died without a male heir she installed her nephew as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a>; Ci'an remained co-regent until her death in 1881. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s defeat in the First Sino-Japanese War, Guangxu launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days' Reform</a> in June 1898 under the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kang-youwei">Kang Youwei</a> and Liang Qichao. Cixi stepped in to stop it and placed him under house arrest for the rest of his life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1900 she backed the Boxers and declared war on the Western powers, which led to the occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> by the Eight-Nation Alliance and her flight to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>. She returned in January 1902 and sponsored the New Policies, a programme more radical than the one she had stopped in 1898, including the abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-examination">examination system</a> in 1905. She died on 15 November 1908, a day after Guangxu, and was succeeded by her chosen heir, his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a>. Her tomb at Zunhua, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>, was looted in July 1928 by troops under Sun Dianying.</p><p>The widely held view is that she was a despot who contributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s slide into chaos and revolution, while some revisionist historians argue that she was scapegoated for structural problems and credit her with pragmatic reform.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Empress Dowager Cixi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1898 the <b>Empress Dowager Cixi</b> suppressed the Hundred Days’ Reform, placing the reformist Guangxu Emperor under house arrest and executing six of his reformist allies; this ended the most ambitious internal attempt at systemic renewal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Empress Dowager Cixi</b> supported the principles of the reform, but feared that sudden implementation without bureaucratic support would be disruptive and that Japan and other foreign powers would exploit any weakness.</blockquote><a href="/movement/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days' Reform · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Xianfeng Emperor died at Rehe in 1861, a palace coup gave power to his widow, the Empress Dowager Ci’an, and to <b>Empress Dowager Cixi</b>, mother of the five-year-old Tongzhi Emperor, who ruled together with Prince Gong.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Foreign troops occupied the palace twice: Anglo-French forces in 1860 during the Second Opium War, and the forces of the treaty powers in 1900 and the following year, after <b>Empress Dowager Cixi</b> fled during the Boxer Rebellion.</blockquote><a href="/work/forbidden-city">Forbidden City · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Zaitian, a nephew of <b>Cixi</b>, took the throne aged three after the Tongzhi Emperor died without an heir, adopting the regnal name Guangxu, &quot;continuation of splendor&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-25">25 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Qing dynasty's European-trained Ever Victorious Army defeated Taiping forces at <b>Cixi</b>, but its commander, the American mercenary Frederick Townsend Ward, was mortally wounded.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-21">21 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Imperial City. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.9,116.41&amp;year=1882" aria-label="Beijing on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Empress Dowager Cixi"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4765 4154h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4765 4154h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.9,116.41&amp;year=1882">What was here in 1882? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
